[00:00] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: Diagram: "agent loop vs harness" showing a model, tools, and context.
AUDIO: 'Agent loop versus agent harness. What is the difference and when you need which one? Look at this basic agent loop. The model asks for a tool. You call the tool. You shove the output back into the prompt and repeat. You can literally'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'write this whole loop in 15 lines of Python code. Now, if your prompt is simple enough, say two or three turns, it works great. But the second you throw a real production task at it, this'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'entire thing blows up in two specific cases. The first one is the context window. So models don\'t remember anything across calls. So every turn has to replay the entire history from'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'scratch. This means every tool is going to be appended. Now, as you reach the limits of your context window, these models get dumber and dumber because the'

[00:50] [scene:problem]
VISUAL: Diagram: "the second way it breaks has nothing to do with tokens" showing tool timeouts and user closing tabs.
AUDIO: 'signal to noise ratio diminishes since you\'re flooding the model with pure noise. So, its reasoning capability tanks. The longer an agent run, the dumber it\'s going to get. Now, context'

[01:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'window management is just half of the problem. There are things like external API drops or times out. So who handles the retries? Then let\'s say if the user has a long running session and they go'

[01:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for an hour walk, where does the state is going to live? Then if your model generates say Python where you safely execute that and if the model or agent decides to drop a table from a database,'

[01:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'who is actually stopping it? None of this actually lives in that 15 lines of Python code. All of this requires scaffolding around the loop. Now, in the rest of the video, I want to talk about'

[01:43] [scene:solution]
VISUAL: Diagram: "so you start adding pieces around it" showing sessions, streaming, retries, sandbox, approvals, context engineering surrounding "the loop".
AUDIO: 'what that scaffolding actually looks like. And then later on, I\'ll show you a really good example of an opensource harness called True Forge, who is also the kind sponsor of this video. Okay, so'

[01:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'around the loop, you need to add things like retry, back offs, isolated code sandboxes, and human in the loop approvals. Also, you need aggressive context management so the window doesn\'t'

[02:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'overflow. This entire infrastructure layer is called harness. Today building that agent loop is trivial but building a really good harness is a very difficult task especially open-source'

[02:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ones. Okay. So with true forge as an example in mind we\'re going to break down the architecture. So the first question that you need to think about is what sits in the prompt before the user'

[02:32] [scene:architecture]
VISUAL: Diagram: "ops", "exa", "deepwiki" tools, "one tool definition", and "context window" with "schema.co" consuming tokens.
AUDIO: 'even submits anything. This is along with the system message. So in standard setup, uh, hooking up a few server with tools automatically loads every single schema up front. That means you\'re going'

[02:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to be burning thousands of tokens even before the user sends a single message. Now, a cleaner alternative is deferred loading. You only pass the server names and say four tiny meta tools, letting'

[03:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the model discover schema on demand. This means you\'re not paying any upfront token cost. However, the model will have to do tool discovery which can potentially increase the latency. Now,'

[03:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the next problem is what happens when your API or tool call returns more data than you actually asked for. Now, the very naive approach is that you simply dump everything into the context of the'

[03:26] [scene:architecture]
VISUAL: Diagram: "get.logs" returning 29,694 characters, decision flow for sandbox or direct context entry.
AUDIO: 'model and let the model figure out what is more important. What a solid harness does instead is stream the raw response straight to disk inside the sandbox. Then it only feeds the model a tiny'

[03:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'preview and uh the local file path and you can let your agent use bash tools to grip the exact traces it needs. This is not only going to save you on token cost'

[03:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but also keep the signal high. Okay, so this brings us to the code mode which is probably the most practical pattern here. So typically a model calls an API, waits for the JSON, reads it into the'

[04:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'memory and then triggers the next API call. But 90% of the time you don\'t need the raw JSON. You are just looking for exact numbers or patterns. So if you do that via regular tool calls, you burn'

[04:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'context tokens and force an LLM to do sloppy mental math. With code mode, the agent writes a concise Python script that runs directly inside the isolated sandbox and then any tool calls inside'

[04:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that script gets bridged back to the harness. So you\'re not sending your API keys to the sandbox. You\'re only getting the final result. This brings up the'

[04:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'question three. Where does all the code actually is running? Most of the shelf setups just package the whole agent into a single Docker container that includes the loop, the secrets and everything.'

[05:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'That works, but it\'s wildly inefficient because every session holds an entire container hostage even when it\'s idle. The better approach is keeping your core loop on your server and treating'

[05:16] [scene:architecture]
VISUAL: Diagram: "where does the code actually run?" comparing "one container per session" vs. "the loop stays on the server" with ephemeral sandboxes.
AUDIO: 'execution environment as ephemeral tools. You spin up a sandbox strictly on demand when code needs to execute and tear it down immediately because the'

[05:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'orchestration loop stays outside your sensitive production tokens never uh are going to be leaked into the execution environment. So sandbox as a tool is not only great for execution but it\'s also'

[05:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'great for security. Okay. Now what happens when a complex task inevitably stretches across dozens of turns. So there are two ways harnesses solve this. First keep the heavy lifting off of the'

[05:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'primary thread completely. Now you spin off a dedicated sub aent with its own fresh window to run noisy research and it only reports back the distilled'

[06:08] [scene:product_reveal]
VISUAL: Text: "so which harness actually does all of this? trueforge open source, MIT from TrueFoundry".
AUDIO: 'answer. Second is complex compaction when the window hits a specific token threshold. A background model summarizes the old returns and swap out the raw history. Just remember compaction is'

[06:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'fundamentally lossy. You are explicitly trading away the fine details to preserve memory. Okay, so this brings us to what I actually wanted to break down today. This is true forge from two'

[06:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'foundry an open source MIT license agent harness. Now what makes it stand out is that it ships all of these mechanism natively. We have deferred schema'

[06:45] [scene:talk]
